nal can link an unreal past condition to a present result. The if-clause stays past perfect, but the present result normally uses would + base verb. Explicit time words help the reader follow the link.","pattern":"If I **had studied** medicine, I **would be** a doctor now.","commonMistakes":["Do not force have into a present result merely because the condition is past."],"examples":["Complete the present result of a mixed conditional: “If she had prepared the visitor guide yesterday, she **would be** free now.”","Complete the present result of a mixed conditional: “If she had restored the old portrait yesterday, she **would be** free now.”"],"skill":"contrast","id":"third-mixed-conditionals-foundation-3","domain":"adjuncts","referenceIds":["bc-third-conditionals"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Present condition, past result","explanation":"A continuing hypothetical state can explain an unreal past result. Use if + past simple for the imagined state and would have + participle for the past result. Match each clause to its own time instead of memorising one fixed pair.","pattern":"If I **were** more organised, I **would have remembered** yesterday.","commonMistakes":["The label mixed describes the time relationship, not an error in tense consistency."],"examples":["Complete the past result of a mixed conditional: “If I were more organised, I **would have prepared** the visitor guide yesterday.”","Complete the past result of a mixed conditional: “If I were more organised, I **would have restored** the old portrait yesterday.”"],"skill":"application","id":"third-mixed-conditionals-foundation-4","domain":"adjuncts","referenceIds":["bc-third-conditionals"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"}],"recap":["If I **had called**, she **would have waited**.","We **might have won** if we had trained more.","If I **had studied** medicine, I **would be** a doctor now.","If I **were** more organised, I **would have remembered** yesterday."],"readingVersion":"core-2026